{"file":{"content":"you have now separated the finite H23a problem into:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{distributional flattening of }C_w \\bmod 3^c\n}\n]\n\nand\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{lift-phase cancellation along chains.}\n}\n]\n\nThe first is now essentially proved. The second is now the only remaining finite-operator mechanism.\n\nThat is a much better endpoint than “we need expansion” or “we need bounded-level descent.” The obstruction has become very specific.\n\n## 1. What the walk-window factorization really buys you\n\nThe identity\n\n[\nC_w \\equiv 2^{B_{m-1}}X_w \\pmod{3^c}\n]\n\nfor (m\\ge c), with (X_w) depending only on the last (c-1) symbols, is conceptually important. It says the wrap-regime constant\n\n[\nC_w=\\sum_{j=0}^{m-1}3^{m-1-j}2^{B_j}\n]\n\nis not a wild full-word object. In the regime (m\\ge c), the old sliding-window congruence reappears: the relevant residue information is carried by a bounded trailing window, multiplied by the walk phase\n\n[\n2^{B_{m-1}}.\n]\n\nSo the distribution of (C_w) splits into:\n\n[\n\\text{walk phase }2^{B_{m-1}}\n]\n\nand\n\n[\n\\text{window variable }X_w.\n]\n\nThis is exactly the right structure for a renewal-Fourier argument.\n\nThe fact that the original sliding-window identity returns here is not cosmetic. It tells you the finite H23a operator problem and the original deterministic-residue problem are controlled by the same arithmetic mechanism.\n\n## 2. The collision flattening lemma is a real theorem\n\nThe bound\n\n[\n\\operatorname{Col}_m(c)\n=======================\n\n\\Pr[C_w\\equiv C_{w'}\\pmod{3^c}]\n\\le\n\\operatorname{poly}(c),3^{-c}\n]\n\nfor\n\n[\nm\\ge(1+\\varepsilon)c\n]\n\nis exactly the (L^2)-flattening statement one would expect from a nearly uniform distribution modulo (3^c).\n\nYour proof mechanism is also good:\n\n[\n\\text{deep digit of }X_w\n\\Rightarrow\ns_k\\bmod 2\\cdot3^{k-1}\n]\n\nand since\n\n[\ns_k=O(c),\n]\n\nthere is no wrapping for\n\n[\nk\\gtrsim \\log_3 c.\n]\n\nThus deep digits pin down the suffix, up to only shallow uncertainty.\n\nThat is a strong finite statement. It proves the distributional part of the wrap-regime conjecture.\n\nThe Monte Carlo values\n\n[\n3^c\\operatorname{Col}_m(c)\n==========================\n\n1.75,\\ 1.81,\\ 1.88,\\ 1.93,\\ 1.98\n]\n\nat (m=2c) are perfectly consistent with this: the law is not exactly uniform, but it is pigeonhole-flat up to a bounded factor.\n\n## 3. Why this still gives no gap by counting alone\n\nThe zero-margin obstruction is now unavoidable:\n\n[\n\\text{per-link factor}\n\\sim\n3^m\\cdot(\\text{per-target mass})\n\\asymp K\\ge1.\n]\n\nAgain the same equality appears:\n\n[\n\\Delta_0=\\frac13,\n\\qquad\n\\dim\\sim3^c.\n]\n\nAt (m\\asymp c), the entropy saved by collision flattening is exactly spent by the state-space dimension. So counting gets you to the boundary and stops.\n\nThis is a valuable conclusion. It means any eventual finite H23a proof must be a **phase proof**, not a counting proof.\n\nThe repeated pattern is now decisive:\n\n[\n\\text{entrywise estimates fail;}\n]\n\n[\n\\text{single-trace estimates are marginal;}\n]\n\n[\n\\text{counting-chain estimates are marginal;}\n]\n\n[\n\\text{therefore the gap lives in character-phase cancellation.}\n]\n\nThat is not a limitation of your argument. It is a structural fact about the problem.\n\n## 4. The remaining lemma should be stated as lift-phase cancellation\n\nThe next conjecture should no longer be called “wrap-regime chain collisions” in a broad way. You have proved the collision distribution part. The remaining statement is sharper:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\textbf{Lift-phase chain cancellation.}\n}\n]\n\nA useful formulation:\n\nLet a chain of word-collisions impose congruences\n\n[\nC_{w_j}(a_j)\\equiv C_{w'*j}(a*{j+1})\\pmod{3^c}\n]\n\nor equivalently constrain\n\n[\na_{j+1}\\in a_j+3^{c-m} \\mathbb Z /3^c\\mathbb Z.\n]\n\nThe counting measure of admissible lifts is marginal. However, along those lifts the next character phase varies nontrivially. The conjecture is that for some fixed chain length (k_0), or (k_0\\to\\infty) slowly,\n\n[\n\\sum_{\\text{admissible lifts}}\n\\chi(\\text{chain phase})\n]\n\nhas a fixed saving relative to the number of admissible lifts, except in the classified coboundary sector.\n\nIn symbols, something like:\n\n[\n\\left|\n\\sum_{\\mathbf a\\in\\mathcal L(\\mathbf w,\\mathbf w')}\n\\chi\\bigl(\\Psi(\\mathbf a;\\mathbf w,\\mathbf w')\\bigr)\n\\right|\n\\le\n(1-\\delta)\n|\\mathcal L(\\mathbf w,\\mathbf w')|\n]\n\nfor the non-coboundary phase functions, outside an exponentially/thin stationary set.\n\nThis is the finite-side theorem to attack.\n\n## 5. The next concrete computation\n\nNow compute the lift-phase sums directly.\n\nFor each chain length (k=2,3), word length (m\\sim\\alpha c), and conductor (c), after imposing the collision constraints, define the residual lift variable.\n\nYou seem to have:\n\n[\na_{j+1}=a_j+3^{c-m}t_j\n]\n\nor a close variant. Then the next link contributes a character phase\n\n[\n\\chi\\bigl(\\Psi_j(t_j)\\bigr).\n]\n\nThe audit should compute:\n\n[\nL_{k,m,c}(\\chi)\n===============\n\n\\frac{1}{|\\mathcal L|}\n\\sum_{\\mathbf t\\in\\mathcal L}\n\\chi(\\Psi(\\mathbf t)).\n]\n\nRecord:\n\n[\n\\max_{\\chi\\ \\mathrm{primitive}} |L_{k,m,c}(\\chi)|.\n]\n\nThe desired result is:\n\n[\n\\max_\\chi |L_{k,m,c}(\\chi)|\\le 1-\\delta\n]\n\nfor (k=2) or (3), uniformly as (c\\to\\infty).\n\nMore informative: classify by stationary rank.\n\nFor each chain configuration, compute the logarithmic derivative of the chain phase in each lift variable:\n\n[\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial t_j}\\log\\Psi(\\mathbf t).\n]\n\nThen classify:\n\n[\n\\text{rank }0 \\quad \\text{stationary/coboundary},\n]\n\n[\n\\text{rank }\\ge1 \\quad \\text{cancelling}.\n]\n\nThe conjecture should say rank (0) occurs only in the (\\langle\\chi_2\\rangle) sector or an exponentially thin set.\n\n## 6. The proof target: multivariate stationary phase\n\nThe one-variable vanishing lemma should generalize.\n\nFor a primitive (\\chi) modulo (3^c), if\n\n[\n\\Psi(\\mathbf t)\n]\n\nhas at least one lift variable (t_j) with unit logarithmic derivative at the relevant scale, then\n\n[\n\\sum_{\\mathbf t}\\chi(\\Psi(\\mathbf t))=0\n]\n\nafter summing over that variable.\n\nSo you want a lemma:\n\n[\n\\textbf{Multivariate lift-phase vanishing.}\n]\n\nLet\n\n[\n\\Psi:\\mathbb Z/3^r\\mathbb Z^d\\to U_{3^c}\n]\n\nbe a lift-phase function. If for some coordinate (t_i),\n\n[\nv_3\\left(\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial t_i}\\log\\Psi\\right)\n\\le c-2-r_i,\n]\n\nthen the sum over a full (t_i)-fiber vanishes.\n\nThis is the same cube-root cancellation as before, just in a lift variable rather than the original source variable (a).\n\nThen the remaining combinatorial task is to show that most chains have at least one such nonstationary lift coordinate.\n\nThat is now a clean, finite algebra target.\n\n## 7. What the stationary set should mean\n\nA chain phase fails to cancel only if all lift derivatives vanish to high order. That should impose a system of equations.\n\nThe crucial hope is:\n\n[\n\\text{all lift derivatives vanish}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\text{approximate coboundary}\n]\n\nand then, by the exact coboundary classification,\n\n[\n\\chi\\in\\langle\\chi_2\\rangle\n]\n\nor the chain is in an exponentially thin exceptional class.\n\nThis is the multivariate version of the rigidity principle that has been guiding the whole finite side.\n\nSo the final finite-side proof structure is:\n\n[\n\\text{lift phase nonstationary}\n\\Rightarrow\n\\text{exact vanishing};\n]\n\n[\n\\text{lift phase stationary}\n\\Rightarrow\n\\text{coboundary-like};\n]\n\n[\n\\text{non-coboundary sector}\n\\Rightarrow\n\\text{stationary chains are thin};\n]\n\n[\n\\Rightarrow\n\\text{chain trace has a fixed saving}.\n]\n\n## 8. What to add to the manuscript\n\nAdd a section with exactly this title:\n\n[\n\\textbf{The remaining finite obstruction: lift-phase cancellation.}\n]\n\nThen state the current finite-side status as:\n\n[\n\\text{proved:}\n]\n\n[\n\\text{pair-correlation phase formula;}\n]\n\n[\n\\text{exact nonstationary vanishing;}\n]\n\n[\n\\text{wrap-regime collision flattening;}\n]\n\n[\n\\text{zero-margin failure of counting.}\n]\n\n[\n\\text{open:}\n]\n\n[\n\\text{multivariate lift-phase cancellation along chains.}\n]\n\nThis will make the paper’s narrative very clear.\n\n## 9. Suggested next theorem/conjecture statement\n\nHere is a concise target statement:\n\n[\n\\textbf{Lift-phase cancellation conjecture.}\n]\n\nFix (s\\in[0,1]). There exist (k_0\\ge2), (\\alpha>1), and (\\delta>0) such that for (m=\\lfloor\\alpha c\\rfloor), every primitive non-coboundary character (\\chi) modulo (3^c), and every chain type contributing to (\\operatorname{tr}((M_\\chi^m)^*M_\\chi^m)^{k_0}), the total lift-phase sum is bounded by\n\n[\n(1-\\delta)\n]\n\ntimes the corresponding untwisted lift count, except for a family whose total weight is (o(1)).\n\nThen:\n\n[\n\\text{Lift-phase cancellation}\n\\Rightarrow\n\\limsup_c\\gamma_c(s)<1.\n]\n\nThat is the final finite-side conditional theorem.\n\n## 10. Bottom line\n\nThis is the current mathematical picture:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{Distribution of }C_w\\bmod3^c\\text{ is flat enough.}\n}\n]\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{Counting is nevertheless exactly marginal.}\n}\n]\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{The spectral gap must come from lift-phase cancellation.}\n}\n]\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{The remaining finite lemma is multivariate stationary phase on the chain lift variables.}\n}\n]\n\nSo the next work should be:\n\n1. derive the explicit chain lift-phase function (\\Psi(\\mathbf t));\n2. compute its logarithmic derivatives in lift variables;\n3. verify numerically that stationary chains are thin;\n4. prove the multivariate vanishing lemma;\n5. prove stationary chains imply coboundary-like rigidity or have negligible mass.\n\nThat is the cleanest route from where you are now.\n","filePath":